---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { site } from '../../site';

// The guide's code is the tested sample's own source, read at build time, so the page and the
// files CI runs never drift apart.
import books from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/books.mjs?raw';
import catalog from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/catalog.mjs?raw';
import dockerfile from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/Dockerfile?raw';
import page from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/page.mjs?raw';
import server from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/server.mjs?raw';
import settings from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/settings.mjs?raw';
import source from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/source.mjs?raw';
import worker from '../../../../../examples/guide-addon/worker.mjs?raw';

const sample = `${site.repoUrl}/tree/main/examples/guide-addon`;

const setup = `
mkdir classics && cd classics
npm init -y
npm pkg set type=module
npm install @lelap/sdk
`;

const files = `
classics/
  package.json
  books.mjs       the books and where their audio is
  catalog.mjs     step 1
  settings.mjs    step 2
  source.mjs      step 3
  server.mjs      serves any step with node:http
  worker.mjs      step 4: steps 1 and 2 on Cloudflare Workers
  Dockerfile      step 4: step 3 in Docker
  page.mjs        a page of your own in the shared style
`;

const runCatalog = `
node server.mjs catalog
# Add-on running: http://localhost:8787/manifest.json
`;

const tryCatalog = `
curl http://localhost:8787/manifest.json
curl http://localhost:8787/catalog/audiobook/classics.json
curl http://localhost:8787/meta/audiobook/classics%3Araven.json
curl http://localhost:8787/stream/audiobook/classics%3Araven.json
`;

const runSettings = `
node server.mjs settings
# Open http://localhost:8787/configure in a browser
`;

const configured = `
http://localhost:8787/p1.eyJvcmRlciI6IlllYXIiLCJzaG9ydCI6dHJ1ZX0/manifest.json
`;

const folder = `
audiobooks/
  Charles Dickens/
    A Christmas Carol.m4b
  Edgar Allan Poe/
    The Raven.mp3
`;

const runSource = `
BOOKS_FOLDER=/srv/audiobooks FILES_URL=https://files.example.org node server.mjs source
`;

const caddy = `
# Caddy gets and renews the certificates on its own, and tells the add-on it is behind HTTPS.
addon.example.org {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787
}

files.example.org {
	root * /srv/audiobooks
	file_server
}
`;

// Open Library's work IDs for A Christmas Carol and The Raven.
const trySource = `
curl http://localhost:8787/stream/audiobook/ol%3AOL32466W.json
curl http://localhost:8787/stream/audiobook/ol%3AOL41081W.json
`;

const workers = `
npx wrangler login
npx wrangler deploy worker.mjs --name classics --compatibility-date 2026-11-01
# prints https://classics.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev
`;

const docker = `
docker build -t classics .
docker run -d --restart unless-stopped -p 127.0.0.1:8787:8787 \\
  -v /srv/audiobooks:/books:ro -e FILES_URL=https://files.example.org \\
  classics
`;

const toc: [string, string][] = [
  ['start', 'Before you start'],
  ['catalog', '1. A catalog'],
  ['settings', '2. Settings'],
  ['source', '3. A source for your files'],
  ['deploy', '4. Deploy it'],
  ['page-style', 'Pages in the shared style'],
  ['next', 'Where to go next'],
];
---

<Docs
  title="Your first add-on"
  description={`Build a ${site.name} add-on step by step: a catalog, then settings, then a source for your own files, then deploy it.`}
  toc={toc}
>
  <h1>Your first add-on</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Build an add-on from an empty folder in four steps: a catalog of public-domain books, a settings
    page, a source that finds your own audiobook files for book pages, and a deploy. Each step runs
    on its own, so you can stop after any of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The code below is <a href={sample}>the guide’s sample</a> in the repository, and its tests run
    in CI on every change. It uses <code>@lelap/sdk</code> 0.5.0 from npm and plain JavaScript, so
    Node runs it with no build step.
  </p>

  <h2 id="start">Before you start</h2>
  <p>You need Node 20 or later and a terminal. Make a folder and install the SDK:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={setup} label="Terminal" />
  <p>By the end it holds these files:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={files} />
  <p>
    The books come first. Two LibriVox recordings on archive.org stand in for your data; every book
    has an ID that starts with <code>classics:</code>, the add-on’s own prefix.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>
      <code>books.mjs</code>
    </summary>
    <CodeBlock code={books} />
  </details>
  <p>
    <code>server.mjs</code> serves an add-on with Node’s own <code>node:http</code>. The SDK gives
    you a <code>fetch(request)</code> function; this file turns each request into one. On Cloudflare
    Workers, Deno and Bun you need no such file.
  </p>
  <details>
    <summary>
      <code>server.mjs</code>
    </summary>
    <CodeBlock code={server} />
  </details>

  <h2 id="catalog">1. A catalog</h2>
  <p>
    A manifest says who the add-on is and what it answers. Three handlers answer it: the catalog
    lists the books, <code>meta</code> gives one book’s details and <code>stream</code> says where
    its audio is. <code>createAddon</code> does the routing, CORS and caching headers, and checks
    every answer against the protocol.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={catalog} label="catalog.mjs" />
  <CodeBlock code={runCatalog} label="Terminal" />
  <p>In a second terminal, ask it what the app will ask:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={tryCatalog} label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    IDs in paths are percent-encoded, so <code>classics:raven</code> is{' '}
    <code>classics%3Araven</code>. The details of a book it doesn’t have answer <code>404</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app only adds add-ons at an HTTPS address, so to try this one on your phone,{' '}
    <a href="#deploy">deploy it</a>: on Cloudflare Workers that takes two commands. Then paste its
    address under <strong>Settings → Library and sources → Add-ons</strong>. The Classics row shows
    up on Home and the books play from archive.org.
  </p>

  <h2 id="settings">2. Settings</h2>
  <p>
    Add <code>configure</code> next to the handlers and the SDK serves a form at{' '}
    <code>/configure</code>. What the listener chooses goes into the add-on’s address, so your
    server stores nothing, and every handler gets it as <code>config</code>. This step keeps step
    1’s handlers and replaces the catalog with one that follows two settings.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={settings} label="settings.mjs" />
  <CodeBlock code={runSettings} label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    The form has the add-on’s name and description, the two fields and <strong>Save</strong>. Saving
    shows the new manifest address with the settings in it, and a button that hands it to the app:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={configured} />
  <p>
    The manifest now says <code>configurable: true</code>, so the app shows{' '}
    <strong>Configure</strong> for the add-on and opens this page. Fields are <code>text</code>,{' '}
    <code>password</code>, <code>select</code> and <code>checkbox</code>; until someone saves the
    form, <code>config</code> is <code>null</code>. Without a <code>secret</code> the settings are
    only encoded, so anyone with the address can read them; that is fine for an order and a
    checkbox. For a password, set a <code>secret</code>: the{' '}
    <a href="/docs/sdk/#configure">SDK guide</a> shows how, and how to check a login before the
    address is made.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every page the SDK serves ends with a line saying who runs it, here “This page is run by
    localhost:8787, not by {site.name}.” Listeners type passwords into these pages, so they always
    know whose page it is. See <a href="#page-style">pages in the shared style</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="source">3. A source for your own files</h2>
  <p>
    So far the add-on answers only for its own <code>classics:</code> books. A source answers book
    pages from other add-ons too: when a listener opens <em>A Christmas Carol</em> from the Open
    Library catalog, {site.name} asks every add-on that takes <code>ol:</code> IDs for streams. Your
    add-on looks the work up and, if one of your files is that book, offers it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The files are your own audiobooks, one file per book, in a folder by author. A web server or a
    WebDAV server serves the same folder over HTTPS, since the app plays audio from HTTPS addresses
    only. Here that is <code>https://files.example.org</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={folder} />
  <p>
    <code>createWorkIndex</code> indexes the files by title and author once, at start.{' '}
    <code>lookupWork</code> turns the ID the app sends into what the index needs: an{' '}
    <code>isbn:</code> as it is, an <code>ol:</code> work’s title and authors from Open Library.{' '}
    <code>index.find(work)</code> returns the files that are that work: a shared ISBN first, else
    the same title and author after folding case and accents and dropping articles, notes such as
    “(Unabridged)” and subtitles. The rules are on{' '}
    <a href="/docs/mapping-files/">Mapping your files to works</a>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={source} label="source.mjs" />
  <CodeBlock code={runSource} label="Terminal" />
  <CodeBlock code={trySource} label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    Each answers a stream that points at the file on your file server, such as{' '}
    <code>https://files.example.org/Charles%20Dickens/A%20Christmas%20Carol.m4b</code>. The add-on
    never serves the audio itself. Once it is deployed (step 4), add its address in the app, search
    for <em>A Christmas Carol</em> and open the book: <strong>My files</strong> is one of its
    sources.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Restart it after adding books.</strong> The folder is read once, at start.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A book in several files</strong> needs a stream with <code>parts</code>, and each part
      needs its length; read it from the tags with a library such as <code>music-metadata</code>.
      Tags are also where ISBNs live: put them into each entry’s <code>ids</code>, and{' '}
      <code>idsFromTags</code> reads them for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep both addresses to yourself.</strong> A web add-on’s streams can’t send a login,
      so anyone with a file’s address can play it, and anyone with the add-on’s address can ask it
      for any book. Share them only with your household, and only serve audio you are allowed to
      share.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Only for yourself?</strong> {site.name} reads a folder on the phone and WebDAV servers
      with a login on its own, and links your files to book pages on the device. A source add-on is
      for files on a server the app can’t reach that way, or for a household sharing one collection.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deploy">4. Deploy it</h2>
  <p>
    An add-on online needs HTTPS and an address that stays the same. Steps 1 and 2 need nothing but
    the SDK, so they run on Cloudflare Workers. Step 3 reads a folder, so it runs next to the files:
    on the server that holds them, in Docker or with Node.
  </p>
  <h3>Steps 1 and 2 on Cloudflare Workers</h3>
  <p>
    A Worker exports the same <code>fetch</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={worker} label="worker.mjs" />
  <CodeBlock code={workers} label="Terminal" />
  <h3>Step 3 in Docker</h3>
  <CodeBlock code={dockerfile} label="Dockerfile" />
  <CodeBlock code={docker} label="Terminal" />
  <p>
    The container answers on port 8787 of that machine only. Caddy in front gives the add-on and the
    folder their HTTPS addresses:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={caddy} label="Caddyfile" />
  <p>
    With nginx, add <code>proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;</code> so the address the
    settings page hands out starts with https. <a href="/docs/hosting/#https">Hosting</a> has more
    on both.
  </p>
  <h3>Add it</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Paste the HTTPS manifest address into the{' '}
      <a href="/docs/manifest-builder/#check">manifest checker</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      In {site.name}, open <strong>Settings → Library and sources → Add-ons</strong>, paste the
      address and add it. Tap <strong>Configure</strong> to choose the settings.
    </li>
    <li>
      Share the address of steps 1 and 2 with whoever you like; keep step 3’s to your household.{' '}
      {site.name} keeps no directory of add-ons; listeners add yours by its address.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Keep the manifest’s <code>id</code> and each stream’s <code>sourceKey</code> the same in new
    versions: listeners’ saved places depend on them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="page-style">Pages in the shared style</h2>
  <p>
    The configure form, the page after it and {site.name}’s own add-ons’ pages share one style, so a
    listener moving between them sees one design: dark, amber buttons, one column that fits a phone.
    The SDK exports it as <code>PAGE_STYLE</code>, one stylesheet with no requests to anyone.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It styles <code>main</code>, headings, links, labels and fields, a <code>.help</code> line
      under a field, <code>.check</code> boxes, <code>fieldset</code>, buttons and{' '}
      <code>.button</code> links, <code>.error</code>, <code>.muted</code>, steps in an{' '}
      <code>ol</code>, a tap-to-copy <code>.copy</code> button and the <code>.sender</code> line.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every page ends with the sender line: “This page is run by {'{host}'}, not by {site.name}.”
      The SDK’s pages always add it, with the host the request came to. Add it to pages of your own
      too. {site.name} never appears as the sender of a page it doesn’t run, since these pages ask
      for tokens and passwords.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For a page of your own, such as help or a sign-in step, put <code>PAGE_STYLE</code> in a{' '}
    <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element and the sender line last in <code>main</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={page} label="page.mjs" />

  <h2 id="next">Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/sdk/">SDK guide</a>: passwords and sign-in, health, badges, errors and tests.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/protocol/">Protocol v1</a>: every route and field, including podcasts, search
      and catalogs in pages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/samples/">Samples</a>: the same kind of add-on in TypeScript with Hono, plain
      Node without the SDK, and Python.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/hosting/">Hosting</a>: Workers, Docker, HTTPS and static files in detail.
    </li>
  </ul>
</Docs>

